  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 201 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nAfter fruitful talks with the wind, an agreement has been reached \nfor an immediate ceasing of its northeasterly onslaught of the \nFlorida Atlantic coast. This calming of the winds shall commence \nearly Thursday morning as pressure gradients finally begin to relax \nacross South Florida. While surface high pressure off the coast of \nthe mid-Atlantic United States will still be quite strong, it's \ninfluence over the winds in South Florida will begin to diminish as \nthe main centroid drifts further east into the ocean. Meanwhile, \nsurface troughing along the northern Caribbean will also begin to \nwash out as a weakening surface low drifts west into the Gulf. This \nwill allow for wind directions to finally change (after about a week \nof northeast flow) to a more easterly direction. Wind speeds will \nlessen to 5 to 8 mph, and a more typical sea-breeze pattern may \nactually be able to develop during the afternoon (although the \nwesterly Gulf breeze will likely struggle to push far inland). \n\nRelating to the pattern aloft, ensemble guidance is in good \nagreement regarding the evolution of the cut-off mid level low over \nthe Bahamas. This feature should finally eject east into the \nAtlantic by end of the day Thursday. This will allow for ridging \nfrom the Gulf to expand across the Florida peninsula through the \nweekend. Guidance is hinting that 500 mb heights may climb into the \n588 to 591 dam range, which would be above the 75th percentile for \nthis time of year. This pattern should result in temperatures \nwarming back up into the mid to upper 80s across South Florida and \noverall less unsettled weather. But, with PWATs hanging around at \n1.6 to 1.8 inches, PoPs will still be around 30% to 40% across most \nof the region. Continued east to northeast flow will keep the \nhighest chances for showers and thunderstorms over interior and \nsouthwest Florida, closer to the Gulf breeze convergence. Overnight, \nisolated to scattered showers will continue to be a nuisance along \nAtlantic coastal areas. Luckily for you trick or treaters on \nHalloween night, the weather is looking quite nice, with only about \na 20% chance of showers along eastern metro areas and light to calm \nwinds from the east.",
